00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.100
that need to be moved.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.342
How do we avoid this being the
next two, three years delay?

00:05:00.342 --> 00:05:01.800
What do you deal
with multi-source.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.399
When you move a
whole data center,

00:05:03.399 --> 00:05:05.190
that includes virtual
machines, but it also

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:07.260
includes some physical servers.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.660
What do you do with
previous deployments

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.480
you did to another cloud when
it was maybe the only option

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.290
available?

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.230
And now that there are more
options, maybe more cost

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.560
effective, how do you take at
mass large amount of workloads

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.604
that are say, at AWS,
and move them into GCP?

00:05:23.604 --> 00:05:25.520
How do you deal with
complex applications that

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.510
are in the data center or SAP,
multi-tier complex workloads

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.880
with large databases which
have very strict downtime

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.345
requirements?

00:05:34.345 --> 00:05:36.720
And then last but not least,
how do you mitigate the risk

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.940
in moving those workloads?

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.310
Many enterprise
customers have told us

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:44.274
that this is actually
a barrier to adoption

00:05:44.274 --> 00:05:45.690
because if something
doesn't work,

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:49.710
I can't afford to
have downtime for days

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.350
without my application running.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.780
So these are the
main challenges.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:59.150
And Velostrata has been
designed from the ground up

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.140
to address these very
exact challenges.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:06.860
It's an enterprise-grade,
field-proven solution that

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.350
actually focuses on
three major use cases--

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:12.120
number one, data
center migration,

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.080
horizontally moving whole data
centers into public cloud;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.545
number two, the ability
to take, in large scale,

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:22.110
workloads that are
running in another cloud,

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.860
say, for instance, AWS, and
move it into Google Cloud;

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.980
and number three, a
specific focus on SAP.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.850
You heard today in the keynote
the partnership with SAP.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.250
We're actually taking it
to the next level in terms

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:38.740
of the migration and
working on SAP migration

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.970
both lift and shift as well
as lift and modernize, meaning

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:45.000
taking their existing
DB2, or Oracle,

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.580
or SQL relational
databases and move them

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.210
into HANA in conjunction with
moving them to the cloud.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:56.100
So let's talk about the product.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.990
How does it really work?

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:01.575
So there are two fundamental
concepts underlying

00:07:01.575 --> 00:07:04.350
the Velostrata technology.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:08.760
Number one, we actually
offer the ability

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:13.980
to take stateful, large
workloads running on-premises,

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.680
and literally within
minutes, have it-- run it

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.490
in the cloud, fully
operational, irrespective

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.740
of the size of the
data that is attached

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:27.480
to that workload or latency
that is across the web.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.090
We call this streaming in
analogy to video streaming.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:33.130
So you don't have to
download entire video

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.890
file to start watching it.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:37.530
So in the same
idea, you don't have

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.390
to replicate all the
data to the cloud

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.760
to start computing
it or consuming it.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:46.391
So the impact of that is that
from a downtime perspective,

00:07:46.391 --> 00:07:47.890
you suffer a few
minutes of downtime

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:50.300
versus sometimes hours or days.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.110
But it also allows you to start
your validation and migration

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:57.220
acceleration much faster
than other solutions.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:01.770
The other fundamental important
concept is agentless design.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.220
And what does this mean?

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.640
This means that we
don't install any agent

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.820
on the workload of the
actual machines that

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:09.690
are being migrated.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:12.690
This turns out to be hugely
important for enterprises

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.190
because it means
that you don't have

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:17.490
to have intimate knowledge
of how the application works,

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:20.370
how to sync it up,
how to orchestrate

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:22.140
multi-tier applications.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.480
And it turns out to
be a huge money saver

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.600
because it need much
less headcount and human

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.410
manual labor in doing it.

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.650
So those two agentless
streaming basically

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:36.520
means much, much faster
and much cheaper.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:38.679
The other third element
is also being safer.

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:39.730
We'll talk about it.

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:42.169
So how does it really work?

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:44.860
Well, so the solution
starts with deploying

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:50.140
a virtual appliance, which
is right here in the data

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.540
source of the data
center or the cloud.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:59.020
And then, there is an automatic,
on-demand and a [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:02.784
deployment of one or more
cloud extensions in the cloud

00:09:02.784 --> 00:09:03.700
region of your choice.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:06.670
It could be multiple
regions, et cetera.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.540
Then let's say that, the
user said-- well, I'm sorry.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:11.290
There's also a one-time set up.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:12.520
You need to set up the VPN.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:14.230
You need to set up the VPC.

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:16.255
And after this set-up
for the infrastructure,

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:18.550
you're ready to start
doing migration.

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:22.205
For instance, a user can
pick using the plugin

00:09:22.205 --> 00:09:23.830
that we have into
the VMware Management

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:24.940
Console, the vCenter.

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:26.360
You can actually
pick one or more

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.270
of VMs or a whole application.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:30.940
And say I want to
run it in the cloud.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:34.000
When this happens,
our right here

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.030
back end appliance, knows
to take control over the VM,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.700
shut it down in a
graceful manner,

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:41.560
take control over his
disk, and essentially

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:43.950
starts the boot
process in the cloud.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:48.500
It establishes a data channel
with a cloud extension,

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.460
which in turn creates a
whole kind of a virtual disk

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:55.540
in the cloud that corresponds
to the remote storage,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:57.040
and then starts the
boot process off

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.520
of that virtual
disk in the cloud.

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:04.660
Now, normally this
would be problematic

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:06.820
if it would be just simple
[INAUDIBLE] this way.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:11.320
But fortunately, we've actually
implemented a very extensive

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:13.810
layer of WAN
optimization that makes

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:17.620
this work in as good performance
as on-prem and sometimes even

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:18.770
faster.

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.590
It includes caching.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:23.660
For instance, all the operating
system images are very similar.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:29.230
We see 95% to 98% similarity on
different or similar flavors.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:33.237
And those can be
completely server locally.

00:10:33.237 --> 00:10:34.820
For data that needs
to be transferred,

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:38.020
we offer network
deduplication, compression,

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.765
predictive prefetch based
on a number of heuristics,

00:10:40.765 --> 00:10:43.940
read-ahead, and so
on and so forth.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:47.140
So the vast majority of the
operations occur in the cloud.

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.200
We also commit all the
write operations locally

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.630
in the cloud using our
storage subsystem that

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:55.780
is highly resilient and
highly available in the cloud.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:58.220
So the bottom line, within
minutes, you're up and running.

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:01.180
We also have an auto
adaptation layer

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.880
that knows to take the instances
and convert them automatically

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.530
to be able to run in the cloud--

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:10.240
Java injection,
licensing enforcements,

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:11.600
and so on and so forth.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:14.290
So the bottom line is, you
have an instance running here

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:17.170
and magically a few minutes
later, it's running,

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.090
fully operational in the cloud.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:23.200
Now, once we do that, in
the background, we kick--

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.650
actually in parallel--
data migration storage

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:27.910
to test all the call data
and start migrating it.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:29.800
But important to
note, the workloads

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.280
are already running
operational in the cloud

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.490
while the data migrates.

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:36.340
You don't have to wait
for it to start running.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.280
While doing this, the
system continues to operate.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:43.990
And when data is fully
migrated, then you

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:47.410
can hit the detach operation
that essentially allows

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:49.870
you to cut the cord and
actually do the conversion

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.340
to completely cloud storage,
and then you're done.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:58.810
Important to note that
while you are at stage two

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:01.260
and before you cut
the cord, so to speak,

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:04.350
you have the option to roll
back instantly within minutes

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:07.590
because we always keep all
the changes synced back

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.900
into the data center,
which is, again,

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.415
turns out to be very important.

00:12:11.415 --> 00:12:14.520
One of our customers, Cardinal
Health, reported about 4%

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.420
of rollback typically
due to misconfigurations

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:20.460
of the network or other
elements that prevent it

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:24.300
from that workload to operate
in a proper manner in the cloud.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:25.950
So knowing that you
have that allows

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.660
you to go at scale
without having

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:31.350
to worry about what would happen
if that workload didn't work,

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:34.430
so very important.

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:37.000
So overall, again,
I showed you how

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.940
do we take a workload that
within literally minutes,

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:41.570
have it operational
in the cloud.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:43.880
And now we'll turn and
see how does this work?

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.607
We need to deal with hundreds
of workloads at the same time.

00:12:46.607 --> 00:12:47.440
Turn it over to Ady.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:55.130
ADY DEGANY: Guys, thanks for
joining us for the session.

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:58.610
So I'll talk about the features
that really make migration

00:12:58.610 --> 00:12:59.940
at scale work.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.520
And one of the
things that we wanted

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:05.630
to ensure that when we
go to the enterprise,

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:08.120
we can fit into
the way things are

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:12.710
being done in this data
center today and augment that

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:15.890
with additional
capabilities for automation

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:19.470
so that you can weave
it into your practices,

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.370
whether it's workflow
management or orchestration.

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:28.280
So just a show of
hands, how many of you

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.080
are using VMware on-prem?

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:33.540
That's great.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:37.230
So, we saw a lot of
customers making investments

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:42.780
over the last 10, 15 years
in their VMware environment.

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:44.680
And that goes beyond
just the compute.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:48.750
It goes into operations
management, and monitoring,

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.010
and health management.

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:53.490
And what we wanted to do is
really take that environment

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:56.040
and augment it in
a natural way so

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.070
that it will reduce
the learning curve

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:02.250
of moving these
workloads to the cloud

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:04.950
or using the clouds in
tandem with that environment

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:07.330
in the hybrid scenario.

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:10.590
So we've built the
management of our solution

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:13.065
with tight integration
into the vSphere platform,

00:14:13.065 --> 00:14:16.380
into the vSphere
vCenter Web Client,

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:20.310
augmenting and extending the
existing objects that we're

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:22.460
going to deal with,
which is a virtual data

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.840
center and the virtual machine.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:29.280
So in the demo that
we'll show in a moment,

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.430
you'll see how that
looks like and work.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.010
Now, when it comes to
large scale, of course,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.350
we're not expecting
users to go and click

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:40.890
on each VM out of your
1,500 VM inventory

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.850
that you have in that cluster.

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:46.600
And this is where we built a
set of tools for automation.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:51.600
So we have a published REST
API that you can go and use

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:54.300
with your favorite
orchestrator, whether it's

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:59.630
vRealize automation, or
whether it is Ansible,

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:03.390
or another solution of choice.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.640
We also offer a
PowerShell module

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.220
for scripting
integration, as well as

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:13.230
a built-in mechanism
for orchestration

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.190
of the large-scale migration.

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:18.946
And this is a feature we
call the Automation Runbook.

00:15:18.946 --> 00:15:23.050
The Automation Runbook really
gives you out-of-the-box

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:28.200
experience of migrating
large sets of VMs,

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.890
and not just individual servers,
but giving you the ability

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:35.910
to describe full stacks,
full application stacks.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:39.330
It can be anywhere from
tens and hundreds of VMs,

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:42.930
as well as controlling the
information on how they're

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:45.090
going to be configured
in the cloud, what

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:47.100
security groups they're
going to go into,

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.840
what tagging strategy
you want to apply.

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:53.400
You can select what instance
steps you are going to use,

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:56.570
what the disk drives, how
they're going to be called,

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:59.070
as well as the ordering
between the VMs

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:02.310
when you're moving the
different tiers in that stack.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:05.310
And we'll see that
all in the demo.

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:09.330
The Automation Runbook comes
with a very simple UI to use.

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:11.940
It also comes with a
REST API, so you, again,

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:18.750
can tie it into your automation
mechanisms and practices.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.430
So we'll dive into
that in more detail,

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:24.330
also in the context of
the migration practice

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:27.510
and how to implement that.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:30.870
Now, one of the things
that come up very often

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:34.860
is that when you're deploying
in a VMware environment,

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:36.750
you get built-in
over-subscription

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.470
of your cluster resources.

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:43.170
So it's very easy to go and
say I want a 12 CPU machine

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:45.690
with 24 gigabyte of RAM.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:47.460
And even if it
doesn't use it, you're

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:49.350
not paying for it any extra.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:51.420
But when it comes
to the cloud, you

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:53.790
get SLAs around the
system performance

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:56.400
and the types of
machines that you get,

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:58.020
but you're also paying for it.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:02.940
So a very important element is
to properly size your machines

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.190
before you migrate them.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:10.560
Now, when we deploy in
a VMware environment,

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.619
we have the tight integration
that I talked about.

00:17:14.619 --> 00:17:16.349
And part of that
tight integration

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:20.740
is also the ability to monitor
your workloads as they operate.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:23.460
So while they're running,
we will monitor them,

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:26.880
we will identify their
utilization of resources.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:29.420
And that will help us
provide recommendation

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:32.490
what instance type to choose.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:34.590
Later on, of course,
after migration,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:40.350
you get the benefits
of the automatic sizing

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:44.520
recommendations that are
provided on an ongoing basis

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.850
once running in GCP.

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:50.740
And of course, for the
sizing recommendation,

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.920
we'll also take into
consideration specific GCP

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:57.760
features, feature like
custom instance types

00:17:57.760 --> 00:17:59.060
is a very important feature.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:02.320
It allows you to
select exactly the size

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:05.650
of machines that you
are going to need,

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:09.610
as well as the consideration
of sustained use discounts

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:14.420
into the cost decisions.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:16.610
One feature that
comes very handy

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:19.010
when you're migrating
a lot of machines

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:23.540
is the ability to validate,
validate your plan,

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:27.780
validate your practice,
validate your configuration.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:32.000
And when it comes to
critical workloads,

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:33.920
sometimes you want
to do it, you want

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:36.350
to do the validations
before you actually

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:37.770
touch your application.

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:39.980
So we have the
ability to snapshot

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:42.890
an entire set of machines that
are running in an application

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:48.350
stack and bring up a thin
clone of that stack in GCP

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:52.670
in an isolated VPC, or in
isolated network subnet

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:54.680
so there's no conflict
with your on-prem,

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:57.380
and allow it to
validate your procedure,

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:00.740
validate the configuration,
validate performance.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:04.340
And you can do that in a click
of a button or an API call.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:07.850
And you can do that without
waiting for any data

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:08.520
to transfer.

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:11.300
And this is the beauty of
the streaming technology

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:15.020
that Issy mentioned earlier.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:20.150
Now, when we go into enterprise
environments, in many cases,

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:22.890
they were built to control--

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:24.920
there were mechanisms
built to control

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:26.880
a lot of the incoming traffic.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:30.950
But when you're migrating,
traffic is outgoing.

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:33.860
And this is where
people typically

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:36.872
struggle in identifying
how much bandwidth they

00:19:36.872 --> 00:19:37.580
need to allocate.

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:39.680
How do they govern
the bandwidth that

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:42.120
is used for migration
versus the other SLAs

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:46.220
that they need to provide
to their organization

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:48.540
because the users are
not moving to the cloud?

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:53.960
So, in order to eliminate
the complexity associated

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:56.390
with deciding how
much bandwidth do I

00:19:56.390 --> 00:20:00.050
give this VM when it migrates,
or that VM when it migrates?

00:20:00.050 --> 00:20:04.370
And what do I do when I have
a data center that now I

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:07.100
want to distribute
across multiple regions

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:09.570
because I have that
capability with the cloud,

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:12.770
and I want to bring the
workflows closer to the users?

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:18.290
So this is where we've built a
governed bandwidth management

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:25.700
mechanism that enables you with
a simple setting at the site

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:30.380
level, decide how much you
want to allocate to migration.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.350
And the system will do
its work automatically.

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:35.870
You don't need to
split it manually

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:38.750
between the different workloads
and the number of workers

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:39.947
that are migrating.

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:46.900
Worth mentioning some additional
enterprise-grade capabilities.

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:51.270
First of all, security,
encryption, network security,

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:52.770
and network policies.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:54.960
The solution has
been built to be

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:58.770
able to work within your
private address space.

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:02.490
There is no need for you to
open internet access for it.

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:06.450
You can operate it
completely within your VPN

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:08.310
and your private address space.

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:11.370
And we support features
like private Google access

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:16.590
so that you don't need to expose
more than you were planning to.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:19.590
We also support features
like working behind NAT,

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:21.990
working behind HTTP proxies.

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.630
So anything that
you are familiar

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:27.000
with from your
environment is something

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.590
that we can work with.

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:31.680
And you don't need to go
through painful changes

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:35.040
to your existing infrastructure.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:40.120
Now, measurement monitoring
is an important piece.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.840
How do you know that
you are maximizing

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:44.490
your available resources?

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:46.020
Can you migrate more?

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.340
Can you migrate faster?

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:51.300
Is the application getting
the performance it needs?

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:55.440
This is all something that
requires a lot of telemetry.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:59.430
And a lot of you know from
VMware deployments on-prem

00:21:59.430 --> 00:22:04.500
that monitoring is a difficult
environment to manage.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:08.460
So we went on and
built essentially

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:12.580
a Velostrata-hosted service
that will perform the log

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:15.780
aggregation,
telemetry collection,

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:19.380
and will give you the results
processed and analyzed

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:24.330
into your vSphere environment
as readily available graphs

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:31.320
without the need to beef
up the deployment on-prem.

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:38.160
So before we delve into the
demo, a little bit on migration

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:42.210
practices and some insights
that we have learned together

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.370
with our partners
and our customers

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:47.670
in the process of migrating
thousands of machines.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:50.310
First of all, it's
very important

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:54.780
to determine ahead of
time what is the inventory

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:56.080
that you want to move.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:57.510
What do you want to move first?

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:59.040
What do you want to move next?

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:02.370
Coordinate in advance with
the application owners.

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:05.250
Coordinate in advance with
the infrastructure team,

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:06.750
with the networking team.

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:09.120
Identify the workloads that
you don't want to move,

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:11.070
so you don't waste time on them.

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:14.520
Identify workloads that
were originally problematic

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:16.380
and maybe remained
on physical machines

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:20.220
because they couldn't
be virtualized before.

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:22.350
This allows you to plan ahead.

00:23:22.350 --> 00:23:24.630
And planning ahead is
important because when

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:27.780
you want to migrate
a lot of machines

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:31.740
when you want to achieve
scale, the best practice

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:34.890
we've seen working
well is to do that

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:37.050
by establishing a pipeline.

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:41.220
Make sure that you always have
machines identified and ready

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:42.090
to move.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:45.360
Create that pipeline, and
you can migrate even hundreds

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:48.550
of machines per day.

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:51.780
And for that discovery
process, we've

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:55.020
been working with our
various partners, partners

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:58.040
like RISC Networks
and Cloudamize,

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:00.720
and others that are joining in.

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:05.010
And we continue to work
with them to optimize.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:09.020
And they can also integrate
well with our Automation Runbook

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:10.970
and REST APIs.

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:13.890
And if you are not integrated
and you want to be,

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:20.310
then this is all public APIs,
and we'll be happy to share.

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:23.040
Now, automation is key.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.620
And we'll show some of
it as part of the demo.

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:31.140
This is a case study.

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:35.130
These are actual measurements
from a customer project.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:38.310
This is a customer
that has migrated

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:40.870
several hundreds of machines.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:44.490
And you can see as we were
working with the customer

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:48.720
to identify the best practice
for them, to build the team,

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:51.750
to build the migration
practice itself,

00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:55.620
we converged with them
on an agile approach.

00:24:55.620 --> 00:25:00.660
The agile approach allowed
them to increase their velocity

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:04.890
eight to 10 times, which
means at some point,

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:10.800
they were able to migrate
about 150 machines in one day.

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:16.440
And what is that sprint approach
or agile approach looking like?

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:19.440
If you have a team,
or you structure

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:21.720
a set of teams that are
dealing with different aspects

00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:25.680
of your environment, you
can create this pipeline

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:28.650
where you spend one
week identifying

00:25:28.650 --> 00:25:31.470
the inventory for
the next sprint,

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:33.510
working with the
application owners

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:37.530
to schedule the migration,
to schedule the validations,

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:41.430
and then prepare the Automation
Runbook, validate it.

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:44.310
The Automation Runbook is
a self-documenting plan,

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:47.190
so you don't need
to do that twice.

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:51.240
And then go into the next
week, and you execute the plan.

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:55.860
The machines migrate and
are live within typically

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:57.690
just a couple hours.

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:00.240
So you can have your
application orders come in

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:04.470
on a predictable schedule,
perform the validation,

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:06.990
and hand over to the users.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:11.850
And after a set of trial period,
you can decide to cut the cord

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:13.380
and complete the migration.

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:16.620
Or if any issues came up
and they are unexpected,

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:18.780
leverage our rollback
feature to be

00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:21.900
able to move the machines
to operate back on-prem

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:25.900
with full consistent state.

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:28.140
So this is an
example of a sprint

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:33.490
that we're migrating about
70-plus machines in one go.

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:38.310
And what you can see here in the
graph is that the blue line--

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:40.590
and don't catch
me on the color--

00:26:40.590 --> 00:26:43.250
is the number of machines
that we're running

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:47.701
and how long it took for
them to actually run and be

00:26:47.701 --> 00:26:48.450
live in the cloud.

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:52.290
And you can see that
within about two hours,

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:56.160
the entire set of machines
across all the migration groups

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:58.530
and tiers were up and running.

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:01.050
Within the next day
and a half, the data

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.330
was migrated in the background.

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:05.160
But that doesn't
prevent the application

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:07.200
from running,
which means you can

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:13.900
be live in the cloud in just
minutes regardless of the size.

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:17.100
So we'll switch over
to a quick demo.

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:18.180
We didn't have much time.

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:21.810
We had a lot to
show, so we're going

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:27.270
to show a time-sped
recorded demo.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:30.960
We have a showcase
downstairs that you

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:34.680
can go in and play with
these different scenarios

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:36.016
interactively.

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:42.740
So first we'll start with
the vCenter integration.

00:27:42.740 --> 00:27:46.560
And I'll go through
that quickly.

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:51.860
So this is the well-known
vSphere Web Client user

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:53.070
interface.

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:55.910
And you can see the
integration through extension

00:27:55.910 --> 00:27:58.430
of the virtual machine object.

00:27:58.430 --> 00:28:01.700
So you can select a virtual
machine, right click on it,

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:03.720
and you'll get the
Velostrata menu.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:06.680
Or you have several
options for the migration.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:09.530
Two notable ones
are the Run In Cloud

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:11.960
option, which allows you
to take any workload,

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:14.930
run it in the cloud in
minutes without having

00:28:14.930 --> 00:28:15.820
to move the storage.

00:28:15.820 --> 00:28:18.710
This is good if you
want to try things.

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:21.050
If you want to test
how your machine will

00:28:21.050 --> 00:28:24.560
work on a machine with
four terabytes of RAM,

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:28.550
how will it work with on a
machine with 128 CPUs, how

00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:33.080
will it work with a GPU or a TPU
that you never had access to,

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:36.260
you can do that without
having to resort to building

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:39.170
your application from scratch.

00:28:39.170 --> 00:28:41.240
And regardless of how
much data you have,

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:44.850
it will be accessible using
a streaming mechanism.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:48.040
The second notable
option is Migrate.

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:51.560
You decided that you want
to move, let's do that.

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:57.200
So we'll click on
Migrate, and you'll

00:28:57.200 --> 00:29:00.810
get a very simple wizard
built into the system.

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:04.490
You'll select your
target, your target VPC.

00:29:04.490 --> 00:29:09.890
You'll select your
target machine type.

00:29:09.890 --> 00:29:12.920
And this is where you can
see the different rightsizing

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:17.780
recommendations coming in,
whether you want the machine

00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:22.100
that best fits the current
provision size on VMware,

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:24.440
or whether you want
one that is based

00:29:24.440 --> 00:29:27.110
on the actual utilization,
which, in this case,

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:31.120
is very low utilization.

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:32.770
Then you'll select
the storage policy.

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:35.630
And we can talk
about that offline.

00:29:35.630 --> 00:29:38.300
Select the disc
type, and that's it.

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:39.430
You're ready to go.

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:41.680
So let's click Finish.

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:43.960
And now we'll see
the integration

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:48.100
into the vSphere interface
from a monitoring perspective.

00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:51.350
There is a portlet that
shows up for that machine.

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:53.560
So we're not creating
a new object.

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:57.160
We're not creating-- you
are not losing the admin

00:29:57.160 --> 00:29:58.910
context for that machine.

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:01.540
And as you can see, within
the vSphere environment,

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:04.390
you can see the status
of the machine running

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:07.970
in the cloud and the
status of its migration.

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:13.720
So this machine has over
a terabyte worth of disks,

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:15.010
about three disks.

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:18.670
And it is up and
running in 11 minutes.

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:20.410
Another thing you
can see in this

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:23.380
is the integration-- the
further integration with it

00:30:23.380 --> 00:30:27.340
into vSphere, which is
integration into events,

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:30.340
and issues, and
alarms so it will all

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:36.710
propagate into your vRealize
operation management.

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:38.900
11 minutes over one
terabyte, it took me

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:40.740
longer to upload the video.

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:46.550
So let's see what happens if
things don't work as well.

00:30:46.550 --> 00:30:48.260
Maybe there's a quota issue.

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:49.910
Maybe the application
owner is not

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:52.580
ready to come in and
do the validation,

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.170
so you need to rollback.

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:59.810
Rolling back is as easy
as moving to the cloud.

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:02.410
So this machine
has been going on.

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:03.760
It's running live.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:05.890
Storage is migrating
in the background

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:07.700
as you can see in the bar here.

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:10.070
And we decided that we
want to move it back.

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:11.770
So another right
click on that VM.

00:31:14.590 --> 00:31:16.820
And we'll select
the Run On-premises.

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:23.660
And that's it, just
a single click.

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:26.760
And what you'll see is that
the system will be back on-prem

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:28.200
in just a few minutes.

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:30.150
Since the system is
running in the cloud,

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:33.720
we constantly
synchronize the changes

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:36.330
that was happening in
the last few minutes

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:39.400
or up to certain
change capacity.

00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:41.220
And we'll replicate
it back on-prem,

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:45.710
to the disks on-prem so you can
move back at any point in time.

00:31:45.710 --> 00:31:47.610
And if you have backup
happening on-prem

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:50.700
it will always backup the
latest version of that machine

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:54.140
while it's running in the cloud.

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:57.430
So this is the integration
into the web UI

00:31:57.430 --> 00:31:58.840
kind of tasting that.

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:01.810
Let's talk about the
Runbook Automation.

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:04.660
Obviously, when you
have 1,500 machines,

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:10.270
and when you have stacks
of 30 machines or more,

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:14.380
you are not going to do
multi-select or do that

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:15.020
manually.

00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:18.460
So this is where a
Runbook Automation

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:19.990
feature comes into play.

00:32:19.990 --> 00:32:25.830
And we'll start with a simple
multi-tier application.

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:27.860
This is the SugarCRM setup.

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:30.920
And you can see this
application running on-prem,

00:32:30.920 --> 00:32:32.570
on the VMware side.

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:35.480
We'll even go and
create some new state,

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:37.610
add some data just
to demonstrate

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:40.190
that you can migrate
immediately without having

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:44.680
to deal with complex
synchronization and cut over.

00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:48.740
So let's migrate
that application.

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:50.570
First, we'll create a runbook.

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:53.480
To create the runbook, you
go into the Velostrata web

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:58.130
interface, select your
source, vSphere or AWS.

00:32:58.130 --> 00:33:00.840
Additional sources
will come later.

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:03.950
You'll select your source
data center or virtual data

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:07.070
center where your inventory
resides, and the target cloud.

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:15.110
We are going to get
the runbook exported

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:19.570
in CSV format, so very easy
to work with in a spreadsheet

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:21.980
editor.

00:33:21.980 --> 00:33:24.970
So let's take that
inventory that was exported.

00:33:24.970 --> 00:33:27.760
A lot of information is
already included there.

00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:29.200
What's the
configuration on-prem?

00:33:29.200 --> 00:33:31.750
What's the naming as well
as default selections

00:33:31.750 --> 00:33:33.440
for the target?

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:36.910
And then next thing we'll do
is filter out that inventory

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:41.410
just to the set that we
want to migrate in this run.

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:45.620
And we will also create order.

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:48.170
This allows you to take
a multi-tier application,

00:33:48.170 --> 00:33:51.220
define the different
components in each tier,

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:54.290
identify the order between
them, and also identify

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:55.100
service probes.

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:57.270
So when a certain
tier is coming up,

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:59.210
you can probe that the
service is available

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:04.530
before you start up the
next set of VMs in the tier.

00:34:04.530 --> 00:34:07.470
So let's take that
CSV file and save it.

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:12.179
Next thing we want to do is
apply rightsizing to that.

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:18.030
So we'll take the CSV file that
we filtered, we'll input it

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:21.580
into the recommendation
engine, and we'll

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:24.639
get rightsizing
recommendations built

00:34:24.639 --> 00:34:30.583
into the entire set of machines
that are in that spreadsheet.

00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:38.489
We'll select the
relevant recommendations,

00:34:38.489 --> 00:34:41.280
copy them over to the
target instance type

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:42.300
that we want to use.

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:49.600
And the runbook is ready.

00:34:49.600 --> 00:34:54.320
Now, as you can see, the runbook
is a self-documenting plan,

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:58.300
which means you don't have
to take all that information

00:34:58.300 --> 00:34:59.890
and keep it in
other inventories,

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:01.450
in other repositories.

00:35:01.450 --> 00:35:03.130
We'll take that
runbook, and you can

00:35:03.130 --> 00:35:05.670
apply additional
information there

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:07.600
like your tagging strategy.

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:10.450
Define your labels, and
they will be applied

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:11.930
when the machines are moving.

00:35:11.930 --> 00:35:14.587
So let's go and start
the migration job.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:34.800
Now, the migration is done
on a full set of machines.

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:38.700
To verify the operation,
the progress, et cetera,

00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:41.670
you go into the
monitoring page, and this

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:43.890
is where you will see
the state of each VM

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:46.600
that is currently migrating.

00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:50.430
Effectively what you will see
the system change your state

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:55.740
as they go through coming up
live, transferring the storage,

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:57.420
and ready to detach.

00:35:57.420 --> 00:35:59.520
When it's ready to
detach, the migration

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:01.240
effectively has completed.

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:09.530
So let's look at
the application.

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:11.600
The application is now online.

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:25.690
So what we have in the
showcase downstairs

00:36:25.690 --> 00:36:29.170
is also a demo of
additional scenarios

00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:34.556
moving from AWS to GCP,
using the Automation Runbook.

00:36:34.556 --> 00:36:35.930
And again, the
Automation Runbook

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:41.470
is fully available both
in the UI and in REST API,

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:43.420
so you can tie it to
your own orchestration

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:48.061
on your own
automation procedures.

00:36:48.061 --> 00:36:49.227
ISSY BEN-SHAUL: Thanks, Ady.

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:53.979
So with the remaining
time we have,

00:36:53.979 --> 00:36:56.270
we want to see how all this
great technology translates

00:36:56.270 --> 00:36:59.150
into actual business
benefits for the customers

00:36:59.150 --> 00:37:02.640
through some case studies
from actual customers

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:05.930
that we've deployed
our solution with.

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:12.280
So we've done numerous
deployments of thousands of VMs

00:37:12.280 --> 00:37:14.840
from on-premises
to public cloud.

00:37:14.840 --> 00:37:18.680
For example, we have one
public sector company in the UK

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:21.890
that actually has been running
into issues with respect

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:27.920
to having to vacate their hosted
data center within six months.

00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:31.350
They were way, way behind
timelines and budget.

00:37:31.350 --> 00:37:34.790
We were introduced
into that scenario.

00:37:34.790 --> 00:37:38.620
And within pretty much six
weeks and about 10x faster,

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:41.380
completed the migration in time.

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:44.160
And there's, as you can
see, different segments,

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.160
interesting segments that
we've been working in.

00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:50.930
Health care is one, financial,
oil and energy, and few others.

00:37:50.930 --> 00:37:56.750
And what comes across is really
three major benefits-- one,

00:37:56.750 --> 00:38:00.045
significant time savings; number
two, significant cost savings;

00:38:00.045 --> 00:38:03.080
and number three, safe journey.

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:06.450
And this is all what
you see in our solution.

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:09.680
Let's take specifically
Cardinal Health,

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.970
which has gone through
an interesting journey.

00:38:13.970 --> 00:38:18.590
They have come to the conclusion
that they need to, again,

00:38:18.590 --> 00:38:21.230
decommission the data centers,
do a move on [? all ?] [? in ?]

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:22.790
of several data
centers to the cloud.

00:38:22.790 --> 00:38:24.165
They started the
journey actually

00:38:24.165 --> 00:38:26.930
with a different
cloud, AWS actually,

00:38:26.930 --> 00:38:32.090
and moved with Velostrata
quite a bit of workloads again.

00:38:32.090 --> 00:38:34.520
They had time constraints.

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:35.960
They have budget constraints.

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:40.040
And basically decided,
after selecting--

00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:43.550
or basically testing
a few solutions,

00:38:43.550 --> 00:38:45.260
to go with Velostrata.

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:47.270
And we were able to
migrate about 1,000 servers

00:38:47.270 --> 00:38:48.020
within six months.

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:50.240
By the way, not all of
it is actual migration

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:51.110
as Ady pointed out.

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:53.240
There's discovery, there's
planning, there's assessment,

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:55.190
getting alignment from
the [INAUDIBLE] owners.

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:56.460
All this come into play.

00:38:56.460 --> 00:38:59.930
But a rough-- a cadence
of 100 servers per week

00:38:59.930 --> 00:39:02.139
or 5,000 servers per
year is something

00:39:02.139 --> 00:39:03.680
we were seeing
consistently happening

00:39:03.680 --> 00:39:07.500
with a lot of our customers
using our solution.

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:10.850
And then midway through their
[INAUDIBLE] journey into one

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:12.100
cloud, they decided--

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:14.480
made a business
decision to switch,

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:17.750
to go to another cloud,
this time to Google Cloud.

00:39:17.750 --> 00:39:21.250
And during that
time, they also used

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:24.710
Velostrata to actually
make all that transition

00:39:24.710 --> 00:39:25.610
very effectively.

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:27.110
And actually as
we speak, they're

00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:31.700
moving hundreds of
machines per week with us.

00:39:31.700 --> 00:39:34.370
And you can see
savings of millions

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:36.110
of dollars due to
the fact that they

00:39:36.110 --> 00:39:39.680
didn't have to deal with
nearly as much head count

00:39:39.680 --> 00:39:42.022
as they would have needed
with other solution.

00:39:42.022 --> 00:39:43.730
We're running a little
bit short in time,

00:39:43.730 --> 00:39:46.667
so I'll just to mention one
more, which is another energy

00:39:46.667 --> 00:39:47.750
company that we work with.

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:50.250
Again, were using
a different vendor.

00:39:50.250 --> 00:39:52.880
It's the blue line or
the blue color there.

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:54.810
And the trajectory
of their completion

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:59.090
was two and 1/2 years for
completing their migration.

00:39:59.090 --> 00:40:02.360
With a 5x acceleration that
we offered in our solution

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:04.760
after they chose
us, they basically

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:07.640
compressed the time to six
month for the same amount

00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:09.400
of workloads.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:12.620
And not only that,
but they also realized

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:16.130
about a million dollars of
savings due to the reduced head

00:40:16.130 --> 00:40:19.400
count that was required
due to the simplicity

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:22.170
and automation of the solution.

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:26.630
So in quick summary,
this is a time

00:40:26.630 --> 00:40:28.660
for enterprises
to make the move,

00:40:28.660 --> 00:40:30.260
not stay behind competition.

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:32.270
If you want to move
at scale, I think

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:33.680
Google Cloud is your choice.

00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:37.490
And we strongly believe that
Velostrata is a right solution.

00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:40.880
For a time perspective,
you get very quick downtime

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:44.360
or very short downtime,
5x labor savings,

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:48.990
or 80%, 5x acceleration
compared to any other solution

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:51.290
out there, and very easy
solution to work with.

00:40:51.290 --> 00:40:52.940
Thank you very much.

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:54.440
[APPLAUSE]

